{"ts": 1789777399.542175, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted\n\n**Specification:** long feature 29, long feature 7, short feature 4\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 0.997\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.55 \u00b1 0.30\n\n### How I got there\n\nI evaluated all 40 features individually, then built up pairs and triples around the strongest performers (~90 evaluations total). Feature 29 was the standout single at 0.831; features 7 (+0.478), 1 (+0.414), and 4 (\u22120.410) followed.\n\n### Why I stopped rather than kept mining\n\nMy in-sample number plateaued around 0.99\u20131.00 across many different triples. That plateau is the tell: the best triple barely exceeds the best single, and the incremental gains (~0.1\u20130.15 from adding features) are about what you'd get from averaging away noise in correlated versions of one underlying signal. More search would have produced a higher in-sample number without a higher true edge.\n\n### The main caveat\n\n**My 0.997 sits below the search-adjusted bar of 1.017.** That bar estimates what an in-sample maximum like mine is worth after accounting for how much I searched \u2014 so my result is *not* statistically distinguishable from what pure selection on noise would produce. This is the single most important fact about this submission, and it's why I didn't forecast anywhere near 1.0.\n\nWhat keeps me from shrinking all the way to zero is some genuine structure in the data:\n- Sign flips degrade performance sharply (0.997 \u2192 0.510 flipping feature 4; \u2192 0.442 flipping feature 7), so directions are well-identified rather than arbitrary.\n- The non-29 features combine to ~0.57\u20130.63 on their own, indicating shared signal that isn't just feature 29 restated.\n- Each component of the chosen triple had independent standalone strength, so this isn't three noise features that happened to align.\n\nBalancing the selection bias against that structure, I centered at 0.55 \u2014 roughly a 45% haircut on the in-sample figure \u2014 with an SD of 0.30 spanning about 0 to 1.1. That wide interval is deliberate: there's a real possibility the edge largely evaporates out of sample, and I'd rather state that honestly than defend the number I searched hardest to find."}
